Downloaded both actual Actions packages and verified their SHA-256 sidecars. Build-info matches except the expected workflow-commit revision; help, shared-library requirements, packaged licenses/session launcher and CPU-baseline/ABI notes match. Both retain x86-64 PIE, RELRO/BIND_NOW, non-executable stack and unwind sections. Both normal CI packaging checks passed.

Local runtime admission used those unmodified Actions ELFs with the same pinned Nix glibc loader/libraries, representative of the prebuilt Nix deployment environment. After warm-ups, balanced A1/B1/B2/A2/A3/B3 groups each ran 20 invocations per workload. Outputs were correct; no data directory was created. Median group results: arithmetic 91.15ms mold / 88.31ms Wild; branching 54.38ms / 54.12ms. Both pass the existing no-slowdown gate. This does not establish GUI/frame-time performance or a general runtime speedup.

The earlier same-input final-link gain remains 2.327s → 1.389s (0.938s saved), not a whole-build gain. Current evidence does not justify changing the Actions default. No compiler/profile/CPU/security tradeoff, product repair, parser-cache change, compiler update or Nix source-package experiment was made in this task.

