## 2026-11-01 — Cached release follow-up and final linker choice

Defaults: **Wild 0.9 for local Linux development; mold for Linux Actions; the existing NDK linker for Android**. Wild won the measured local final-link comparison by 0.938s (2.327s → 1.389s). Actions showed no qualified whole-job advantage over mold, so keep its existing default. No further Actions linker runs are queued. This is a practical choice from available evidence, not proof that mold is the fastest warm Actions linker. Nix source-package task 3.2 is a separate scope.

Repeated the unchanged Wild candidate `85e9e924` with publication disabled. The runner image and normalized native-package download/version lists match the cold run. Build-info verification, packaging and artifact upload passed; no Actions test suite ran.

| Same Wild release recipe | Cold | Cached repeat |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Whole Linux release job | 82m25s | 40m10s |
| Native build step, including helper | 80m05s | 38m11s |
| Cargo-reported optimized release | 78m28s | 37m55s |
| Helper compilation | 1m33s | 14.82s |
| Compilation cache | Miss; saved 2.439 GB | Exact hit; restore/validation 55s; save skipped |

Observed saving: 42m15s, or 51.3% less whole-job time in this pair. All 4,500 unchanged source timestamps restored. External dependencies were reused, but five helper crates and 17 release project crates still rebuilt. The largest release units were Interface 15m21s, Engine 11m51s, Cell 11m43s and Transport 10m27s; their execution overlaps. Anicca rebuilt too, consistent with the previously reproduced generated-parser timestamp problem; this run did not independently trace that cause. The saved parser/cache-key proposal remains unapplied.

Two builds total establish this cache observation: the first saves inputs and the second restores them. The exact-hit archive is immutable and was not replaced, so another run does not automatically repair its generated-output invalidation. Pinned [Wild 0.9 does not implement incremental linking](https://github.com/wild-linker/wild/blob/0.9.0/README.md). These savings come from Cargo artifact reuse, not binary surgery. Actions excludes final executables and linker state, and disables Rust incremental compilation. Local unchanged builds can retain the executable and skip linking; local dev-profile checks enable Rust incrementality, while `cargo xtask dev` runs the release profile. No warm mold control was run, so this follow-up does not rank warm linkers.

Source: [cached Wild release](https://github.com/lince-social/lince/actions/runs/41359225160). Receipts: `target/build-iteration/task-3-1/warm-result.json`, `wild-warm.log`, `wild-warm-status.json` and `wild-warm-timings/`. No production code, release flags or linker configuration changed in this follow-up.

## 2026-11-01 — Task 6: shared stable Rust updates

Use one exact version in `rust-toolchain.toml`: upgraded 1.96.0 → 1.99.1, the official stable manifest dated 2026-10-15. The minimal profile explicitly retains rustfmt and Clippy for development. Removed duplicate Actions/Android versions and the script's hard-coded `stable` fallback. Native/default/Android Nix shells read this pin; Nix source packages now use the same compiler through pinned rust-overlay metadata instead of the older nixpkgs compiler. Prebuilt Nix flakes include the toolchain file too. Specialized ESP firmware toolchains remain independent.

Automatic updates run daily at 06:17 UTC inside the existing Linux workflow. When the official stable version changes, the updater refreshes only rust-overlay metadata, checks Nix availability, installs/verifies the exact rustup compiler, and stages a new pin. It dispatches the existing Linux checks and both Android architecture checks against main, applying the candidate version only inside each job's checkout. [GitHub's dispatch API](https://docs.github.com/en/rest/actions/workflows?apiVersion=2022-11-28#create-a-workflow-dispatch-event) returns the exact dispatched run IDs directly; the controller waits for those runs and verifies their required compilation jobs and base commit. The committed pin remains unchanged until both workflows pass and main still equals the tested base. Only then are the toolchain pin and flake.lock committed and fast-forwarded. Failed or skipped checks and concurrent main changes preserve main. Candidate checks have separate concurrency groups and cannot package, sign, publish or deploy binaries.

Validation runs on main so its compiled-input cache is eligible for later normal builds: [GitHub's branch restrictions](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/dependency-caching#restrictions-for-accessing-a-cache) prevent main from using a temporary child branch's cache. The earlier temporary-branch design was replaced before deployment for this reason. No hosted speedup is claimed. Explicit dispatches run the checks while the token's push avoids recursively starting releases, following [GitHub's workflow-trigger rules](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/write-workflows/choose-when-workflows-run/trigger-a-workflow). Subsequent normal release runs use the new compiler. No third workflow, approval bot, external credential or repository-setting change was added. The schedule activates after the workflow reaches the default branch.

Manual alternative: `nix develop .#interface -c python3 -B institute/scripts/update-rust-toolchain.py`; add `--check` to report availability without changing files or installing tools. Failed preflight/install preserves the existing pin; detected pin edits during preflight are left intact. Existing Cargo dependencies and other Nix input pins stay unchanged. An already-current update changes no files or cache inputs. Re-enter the shell after an update. Normal build commands do not perform network updates.

The first warnings-denied Linux check found Rust 1.99's new `fetch_update` deprecation in vendored Smithay. Replaced its call and six Park calls with `try_update`. Rust 1.99.1's standard-library implementation defines the old method as a direct call to the new one; source guards confirm the seven files differ only by that method name. Closures, memory orderings, overflow/error handling and behavior are retained; no warning was suppressed or pre-existing product defect repaired.

Validation completed: 15 updater/reader/candidate tests, nine automatic-update fixture tests, seven existing cache-identity tests, real isolated Nix/rustup update plus unchanged repeat, Actionlint and ShellCheck. Fixtures cover main-scoped candidate dispatches, byte-identical pin replacement, source-setting preservation, rejected local/PR/older candidates, failure on either platform, skipped compilation jobs, concurrent main changes, unexpected source edits, unchanged updates and execution-context guards. Required-job predicates also pass actual Actions metadata and wrong-commit/skipped-job probes. Actual Nix and rustup compilers report identical version/commit/LLVM; all four supported Nix platform toolchains evaluate to 1.99.1. Native/Android shell selection, both source-package compiler recipes and the packaged-flake toolchain evaluate correctly. Full Linux native/Park/tools and headless warnings-denied Cargo checks pass. Shared i18n Rust code passes the same checks for both Android architectures; this is not a full mobile/NDK/APK check.

Release optimization, CPU, hardening/unwind settings, linker defaults and Actions test coverage were unchanged by task 6. Compiler changes require an initial dependency recompilation; no compilation-speed or optimized-runtime improvement is claimed. No optimized Lince rebuild, hosted Actions run or task 3.2 linker experiment was started in task 6. No applicable pipe-prefixed requirement was found. Receipts: `target/build-iteration/task-6/`.

Sources: [official stable manifest](https://static.rust-lang.org/dist/channel-rust-stable.toml), [Rust 1.99.1 atomic implementation](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/1.99.1/library/core/src/sync/atomic.rs), [rust-overlay toolchain-file support](https://github.com/oxalica/rust-overlay#cheat-sheet-common-usage-of-rust-bin).

## 2026-11-01 — Task 3.2: Nix source-package linker choice

Owner-directed choice without new testing: use pinned Wild 0.9 through the Clang driver for x86-64 Linux headless and UI source packages. Existing same-input optimized links favored Wild (2.327s mold → 1.389s Wild), with prior correctness/runtime/package gates passing; that evidence informed the choice but does not qualify this Nix recipe or Rust 1.99.1 artifact. Other platform linkers remain unchanged. Release optimization, CPU, unwind/hardening and package behavior settings are retained. No Nix source-package benchmark, build or runtime test was run for this selection. Task 3.2 is closed by the owner's explicit instruction to choose without testing.

## 2026-11-01 — Task 1.1: Park compiler-cache process

Decision: retain the existing cache-process default for Park protocol iteration. In balanced A1/B1/B2/A2/A3/B3 blocks, normal shared-process totals were 13.25/7.34/6.94s; independently started private TCP-process totals were 8.49/7.19/7.24s. Medians 7.34 → 7.24s save only 0.10s, below the predeclared 0.5s/5% gate; the candidate is not consistently faster. No useful speedup is claimed. The first control also rebuilt Anicca/helper units during local settling, which favored the candidate comparison; it still failed the gate.

Each complete block includes Nix shell/cache startup, an equivalent production input-duration edit, Cargo check and the normal `cargo xtask park protocol-check`, then restoration/check/protocol restart and owned-cache shutdown. All 185 protocol cases pass each time; two pre-existing ignored cases remain. The private process uses the same disk cache, compiler, flags and Cargo jobs, starts outside Cargo and is the only process stopped. Existing shared processes and caches are preserved. Its final sample received four Rust requests, all non-cacheable because Rust incrementality was active: moving this work to a separate cache process did not remove the editing bottleneck. Initial helper/test preparation (2m27s/1m37s) and check preparation (1m09s) are separate setup measurements, not iteration gains. The earlier one-compiler/queued-request observation remains setup evidence only. The later task 1.2 preflight also invalidates this same-directory multi-server arrangement: sccache explicitly supports only one server per local directory. Preserve the raw results as observations, not qualification of a safe reusable recipe. Probe bytes were restored exactly. This covers protocol editing, not native compositor acceptance. Receipts: `target/build-iteration/task-1-1/`.

## 2026-11-01 — Task 1.2: shared-Interface compiler-cache process

Decision: reject the proposed same-directory private server and retain the existing default. [sccache 0.15.0's local-storage contract](https://github.com/mozilla/sccache/blob/v0.15.0/docs/Local.md) permits only one server per cache directory; concurrent servers can race and cause spurious build failures. This invalidates the candidate before a performance promotion, regardless of observed CPU utilization. A separate directory would change the retained-cache comparison and was not tested or added as another task. Do not repeat the invalid arrangement.

Actual `cargo xtask dev --help` optimized preparation passed on Rust 1.99.1 with opt-level 3 and incrementality off: initial population 55m15s; subsequent release settling 5m44s. After settling, unchanged check/restart took 0.92/1.27s. These are preparation/freshness observations, not a before/after iteration gain. Application help bytes matched after excluding the launcher's variable incremental-snapshot maintenance messages; no application data directory was created. The timed six-block comparison and source-content probe never started. Stopped only the owned preparation process group/compiler request; private servers were already stopped. Shared servers and disk caches were preserved. No compiler-cache implementation was promoted. Receipts: `target/build-iteration/task-1-2/`.

## 2026-11-01 — Task 2.1: development Interface512 versus 256

Decision: retain 256. The affected launcher-stage edit/check/restart plus restore/check/restart medians were 7.5873s at 256 versus 7.5819s at 512: only 0.0054s / 0.07% saved. Balanced A1/B1/B2/A2/A3/B3 samples: 256 = 7.587/7.583/7.689s; 512 = 7.683/7.534/7.582s. The candidate fails both the 0.5s/5% useful-saving threshold and consistency gate. No useful performance gain is claimed; no default changed.

Scope was narrowed at preflight: normal `cargo xtask dev` explicitly builds the application with the release profile, so a dev-profile codegen override cannot accelerate that optimized app build. Check-only compilation emits metadata without LLVM code generation. Tested the stage the setting affects using `cargo --config profile.dev.package.lince-interface.codegen-units=256|512 check --locked -p xtask` and `cargo --config profile.dev.package.lince-interface.codegen-units=256|512 xtask --help`. Each block includes Nix startup and four commands; original-source preparation is excluded. This is a launcher-stage result, not a complete UI iteration speedup. Its failed stage gate does not justify more costly full-app repetitions or a new default.

Both profiles compile without warnings; all twelve edited/restored check and launcher rebuild pairs detect the Interface change and preserve exact helper help output. Source/build-input guards pass, equivalent f32 literal probes are restored byte-for-byte, and shipping profile/optimization/CPU/unwind settings remain unchanged. Final normal-default UI and helper Cargo checks pass with warnings denied; default helper help and the unchanged optimized application help also pass. The optimized artifact SHA-256 is unchanged by the codegen experiment, and its help path creates no data directory. The report parses and diff checks pass. Existing behavior-test failures were not repaired or rerun. No applicable pipe-prefixed build requirement was found in Lince.lingua. Receipts: `target/build-iteration/task-2-1/`.

## 2026-11-01 — CPU parallelism follow-through

Decision: retain automatic Cargo jobs and the current cache default. The completed P02 command explicitly used `--jobs 4` on an eight-CPU machine. That historical run compiled the full session/UI graph in 27m44s; its one settings-initialization test took 0.00s. The explicit cap limits Cargo concurrency, but does not establish the cause of the observed 15% CPU usage. No repository/global Cargo job override was found. Current warm P02 rebuilds already peak near all eight cores, including with the four-job cap; the Cargo cap did not impose a four-core ceiling across all observed compilation/linking stages.

| Balanced complete edit/rebuild/test + restore/rebuild/test comparison | Control median | Candidate median | Decision |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Current sccache versus bypass, automatic jobs on both | 17.197s | 17.146s | Only 0.051s / 0.30% saved; inconsistent samples. Keep cache. |
| Explicit four jobs versus normal automatic eight, same cache | 15.881s | 16.142s | Automatic 0.261s / 1.65% slower in these warm samples; neither supports changing the default. Keep automatic. |

Each comparison used three balanced blocks per side in A1/B1/B2/A2/A3/B3 order, including Nix startup, with identical compiler/profile/flags and guarded source inputs. All 24 timed selected-test invocations passed; no suite was run. Equivalent production private-file-mode literals forced Park recompilation and were restored byte-for-byte; restored SHA-384 matches the owner's P02 receipt. Two initial 53s artifact-preparation runs and unchanged settling are excluded from timing. These warm results are not a measured speedup against the historical initial dependency compilation.

Sampled compiler/linker CPU peaks reach approximately eight cores with and without the cache. Roughly 72% of samples in the cache comparison used less than 1.5 compiler/linker cores, including serial compilation and startup/idle intervals; removing the wrapper leaves this pattern unchanged. Sampling is not complete CPU accounting or a frontend/I/O attribution profile. High peak utilization does not imply all stages can run concurrently.

The existing default daemon still carries inherited Cargo jobserver variables, and two pre-existing endpoints refer to the old cache directory. Neither daemon was stopped, replaced or given another concurrent server. Inherited variables alone do not prove throttling of this warm incremental workload. The conditional separate-directory cache-server correction was not started because bypass failed the complete-workflow gain gate; no new cache bootstrap was promoted. This does not qualify uncached dependency code generation or claim that the earlier release-preparation constraint is fixed.

Common Nix/xtask and Linux/Android Actions entrypoints have no manual job cap to remove. Actions does not configure this local sccache daemon. No change was propagated to Android/Actions without a demonstrated affected-workflow gain. Final `cargo check --locked -p lince-park --no-default-features --features session --tests` passed with warnings denied (5m02s initial metadata preparation). The report parses and diff checks pass; no applicable pipe-prefixed build requirement was found in Lince.lingua. No new release artifact, Android package or Nix source package was built in this pass. Shipping settings and product behavior remain unchanged; no speculative crate restructuring or product repair was undertaken. Receipts: `target/build-iteration/parallelism/`, including `jobs/`, CPU samples, exact commands and restored-source checks.

## Earlier development-codegen qualification

Earlier R59: native Interface512 shared edit/restoration cycles improved 105.549 → 79.254 s (24.91%); setup cost 214.60 s. Native control recorded 450 passes/10 failures and a 600 s timeout. Four failures were corrected socket-path fixture errors; six behavior cases and the post-catalogue timeout remained unresolved in that run. That different native-test workflow never qualified for adoption; task 2.1 above rejects the setting for the current launcher workflow. These historical behavior failures are not queued repairs. The prior failures involved two customization fixtures and Practice rows, mail permissions, vocabulary confirmation and shader completion; no repair work was undertaken for the current build plan. This is distinct from the rejected Park test-only 512 setting.

## Not queued without evidence

Storage/RAM/no-CoW, Crane packaging reuse, narrower Nix sources, feature unification, asset separation, broader nextest and further compiler flags lack a demonstrated large common-workflow opportunity. Storage requires owned file-I/O attribution; package reuse requires repeated dependency recompilation; source filtering requires generator/path/input inventory and complete installed Trails content. Initial inspection is not a speedup.

Simulation policy separation and Action serialization are architectural proposals, not proven build wins. A simulation prototype must preserve automatic enforcement, all nine roots, no-Cell execution, worker placement and session/replay/campaign/preview identity binding. Action changes must preserve its live schema, strict input rejection and optimized behavior. Complete effective-input identity must cover resolved features/config/profile, included/generated/local source and native tools/flags; retain fail-closed behavior and avoid Cargo private fingerprints as a stable API. These contracts gate affected proposals; they do not justify speculative rewrites.

Remote infrastructure, build-system migration, additional recurring automation and PGO/BOLT require their own concrete objective/resources; none is queued. The explicitly requested Rust update automation is implemented above.

## Rules for a selected experiment

Record hypothesis, exact pins/commands, source guards, useful-saving threshold, bounded samples/time, setup/cache state and rollback before changing code. Benchmark one factor at a time, sequentially, with at least three balanced uncontaminated samples per side for a performance claim. Keep all samples, ranges and exclusions; separate checking, compilation/linking, loading, execution and one-time setup.

Require useful complete-workflow gains, preserved correctness/privacy/recovery and final binary performance. Verify relevant source additions/deletions, dependency/features/tools/native invalidation, and the actual normal command before adoption. Full physical/session acceptance is required only where affected. Restore probes/rejected changes and record adopt, scoped opt-in, reject or conditional deferral. Validate interactions in the useful combination; never add separate gains or attribute global pressure to owned builds.
}
